---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Insiculous 2D — the engine — Be Insiculous"
  description="Insiculous 2D: a 2D game engine written in Rust — wgpu rendering, hand-written ECS, a dockable editor — also a proving ground for agentic coding tools."
>
  <p class="section-label">the engine</p>
  <h1>Insiculous 2D</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    A 2D game engine written in Rust — roughly 57,000 lines across an
    11-crate workspace, shaped by building actual games with it. Six so far.
  </p>

  <section>
    <h2>Architecture</h2>
    <p>
      The workspace splits into focused crates — <code>ecs</code>,
      <code>renderer</code>, <code>physics</code>, <code>input</code>,
      <code>audio</code>, <code>ui</code>, <code>editor</code> — tied
      together by <code>engine_core</code>. The ECS is hand-written and
      deliberately simple: per-type storage with generational entity IDs,
      events, resources, and systems. No archetype sorcery — it's built for
      debuggability and testability over raw throughput, and every game so
      far has run comfortably on it.
    </p>
    <p>
      A game is one trait: implement <code>update()</code>, opt into the
      hooks you need, call <code>run_game()</code>. Swap that single call
      for <code>run_game_with_editor()</code> and the same game boots inside
      a dockable scene editor — inspector with undo/redo, gizmos, entity
      picking, play/pause/stop with full world-snapshot restore. Scenes and
      prefabs are RON files, which is how Breakout's levels are authored.
    </p>
    <a href="/images/pong-in-editor.png" class="shot-link">
      <img
        src="/images/pong-in-editor.png"
        alt="Insiculous Pong running inside the Insiculous 2D scene editor"
        width="1288"
        height="885"
        loading="lazy"
      />
    </a>
    <p class="caption">
      Insiculous Pong running live inside the scene editor.
      <span class="caption-hint">Tap to open it full size.</span>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Rendering</h2>
    <p>
      wgpu with WGSL shaders. Sprites are instanced and CPU-batched per
      texture with depth sorting; everything renders into an HDR
      (<code>Rgba16Float</code>) target and passes through a configurable
      bloom chain — extract, half-res separable Gaussian ping-pong,
      tonemapped composite. Emissive sprites and particles glow for free.
    </p>
    <p>
      The sprite shader evaluates SDF shapes directly — rounded rects,
      circles, borders — with zoom-independent antialiasing, and a separate
      line pipeline feeds the same HDR target, which is what makes the
      signature spring-mass deformable grid ripple and bloom under the
      gameplay. Text is CPU-rasterized with per-glyph GPU texture caching
      and per-locale fonts; tilemaps batch through the same sprite pipeline.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Tech stack</h2>
    <p>
      Rust throughout: <code>wgpu</code> for graphics, <code>winit</code>
      windowing, <code>rapier2d</code> physics, <code>rodio</code> audio,
      <code>gilrs</code> gamepads, <code>glam</code> math, <code>fontdue</code>
      text, RON + serde for scenes and data. Desktop today — Vulkan, Metal,
      or DX12.
    </p>
    <p>
      The design rule from day one: <em>everything must be CLI-testable</em>.
      Over a thousand tests run headless without a GPU, which is also what
      makes the codebase workable for coding agents. Browser export
      (WebAssembly) is designed and on the roadmap but not shipped yet —
      which is why the embed slots on this site are still placeholders
      rather than play buttons.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Agentic-coding proving ground</h2>
    <p>
      The engine is co-developed with AI coding agents under a documented,
      fairly strict process: per-crate instruction files and live tech-debt
      ledgers, a coordination protocol so parallel agents work disjoint
      crates without collisions, and hard guardrails — never claim a test
      passed without running it, never weaken a test to get green, stop
      after two failed attempts instead of thrashing.
    </p>
    <p>
      The distinctive piece is <strong>adversarial cross-model review</strong>:
      one model authors a plan or a diff, a different vendor's model attacks
      it headlessly, and every numbered finding gets an explicit accept or
      rebut. Findings must name a concrete failure scenario — "this might
      have issues" doesn't count as a finding.
    </p>
    <p>
      There's no leaderboard and no pass-rate percentage, deliberately. The
      evaluation is whether an agent's work survives review, the test suite,
      and actual play — judged by the one human who has to live with the
      codebase afterward. The provenance is all in the git history: commits
      carry co-author trailers from the models involved, and per-agent
      branches make approaches comparable on identical tasks.
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .lede {
    font-size: 1.2rem;
    color: var(--text-bright);
    max-width: 38em;
  }

  section p {
    max-width: 46em;
  }

  section img {
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 8px;
    margin-top: 0.5rem;
  }

  /* A 1288px screenshot of a dockable editor is unreadable scaled into a phone, so the image links to itself. */
  .shot-link {
    display: block;
  }

  .shot-link:hover {
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  /* Only worth saying where the image is actually too small to read. */
  .caption-hint {
    display: none;
  }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .caption-hint {
      display: inline;
    }
  }

  .caption {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    color: var(--text-dim);
    margin-top: 0.5rem;
  }
</style>
